From 523bc71d92410945456c348dd1233cb3b439316b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yin Li <kevinli.ai.work@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 01:27:39 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] [Core] Sampled-logprobs-only transport for
 /inference/v1/generate return_token_logprobs
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Follow-up to the return_token_logprobs field. With per-token objects gone
from the response, the remaining cost of asking for the sampled token's
logprob is the per-request path that starts in the scheduler: one
LogprobsLists (token ids, logprobs, ranks: three numpy slices) per request
per step, msgpack-encoded into EngineCoreOutput, then decoded, converted
with .tolist(), detokenized and appended into FlatLogprobs by the output
processor. On an 8-core host serving Qwen2.5-0.5B on one L20 (256-token
rollouts), keeping the D2H copy and tolists() but skipping that per-request
slicing restores 1.00x of no-logprobs throughput at concurrency 256 and
0.96x at 64, from 0.85x / 0.88x — the D2H itself is free at this scale.

This adds SamplingParams.sampled_logprobs_only (requires logprobs == 0).
For such requests the scheduler emits EngineCoreOutput.new_sampled_logprobs,
one float per new token read from column 0 of the step's logprobs array
(the sampler stores the sampled token first), instead of new_logprobs; the
output processor keeps a plain float list (and the cumulative logprob) and
never builds Logprob entries or detokenizes; CompletionOutput.sampled_logprobs
carries the values. /inference/v1/generate sets the flag for
return_token_logprobs when logprobs == 0 and reads the list directly (same
-9999.0 clamp); with logprobs > 0 the previous flat-representation path is
kept. The new EngineCoreOutput field is appended last; the Rust engine-core
client mirrors it as an opaque optional field (it already ignores appended
fields).

Tests: LogprobsLists.sampled_logprobs column selection, processor
accumulation and cumulative logprob, SamplingParams validation; the
endpoint tests from the previous change exercise the new path end to end.

Signed-off-by: Yin Li <kevinli.ai.work@gmail.com>
Co-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
